{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": ".NET Framework: What Is a Form? "}, {"type": "position", "content": 7}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear and use in the .NET framework forms."}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Understand important information about client software for the identity metasystem in spoken or written documents."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.   Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages."}]}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n5le01ob07re1img01.jpg"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Future With Will, Going to, the Present Continuous and the Present Simple in Negative Form"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We can make reference to future events in <b>negative</b>. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p><strong>WILL </strong></p> <p>The use of the form <strong>will</strong> does not change when it is presented in its negative form. Remember that you use the form <strong>will</strong> to talk about future opinions, predictions, spontaneous decisions, and promises.</p> <p>To create <strong>negative</strong> sentences, you have to follow this structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;Subject + <strong>won&rsquo;t (or will not)</strong> + verb (base form) + complement</p> <p>&nbsp;For example:</p> <p><strong><i>He won&rsquo;t</i></strong><i> learn anything about computers in that company.&nbsp;&nbsp; </i></p> <p><i>Shena <strong>will not</strong> miss today&rsquo;s meeting.</i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>GOING TO</strong></p> <p>It is important to take into account that you use <strong>going to</strong> to talk about future predictions based on the present or plans you&rsquo;ve already decided on.</p> <p>&nbsp;To create <strong>negative</strong> sentences, you have to follow this structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;Subject +<strong> am/is/are </strong>+ <strong>not</strong> + going to + verb (base form) + complement</p> <p>For example:</p> <p><i>I am <strong>not going to</strong> write using that language; it is too difficult.&nbsp; </i></p> <p><i>She<strong> isn&rsquo;t going to </strong>come to the meeting.</i></p> <p><strong><i>&nbsp;</i></strong></p> <p><strong>PRESENT CONTINUOUS </strong></p> <p>Keep in mind that the <strong>present continuous</strong> tense can express plans and arrangements in the near future only when they are already scheduled to take place in a specific moment, day, or hour.</p> <p>To create <strong>negative</strong> sentences, you have to follow this structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">Subject + <strong>am/is/are</strong> + <strong>not</strong> + verb (-ing form) + complement</p> <p>&nbsp;For example:</p> <p><i>We<strong> are not meeting</strong> until the software is ready and approved.</i></p> <p><i>I <strong>am asking</strong> the investors in our next meeting what they think about the new product.</i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>PRESENT SIMPLE</strong></p> <p>Remember that you can use the <strong>present</strong> <strong>simple</strong> tense to talk about situations that are scheduled or already arranged.</p> <p>To create <strong>negative</strong> sentences, you have to follow this structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">Subject + <strong>do/does</strong> + <strong>not</strong> + verb (base form) + complement.</p> <p>For example:</p> <p><i>The engineers <strong>do not approve</strong> the software until tomorrow. </i></p> <p><i>The meeting <strong>does not finish</strong> until 9 p.m.</i></p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I'm studying so hard, but I'm not ready for the next Monday's exam. \t"}, {"type": "example", "content": "You will pass the exam."}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Adverbs of Manner"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p>You use <strong>adverbs</strong> to give information about how something happens. Today, we will check the <strong>adverbs of manner</strong>.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>When we want to show the way something takes place, we use the <strong>adverbs</strong> of <strong>manner</strong>. They are generally placed after the main subject or object of the sentence or at the end of the clause.</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Most of the adverbs we use end in <strong>-ly</strong>, but there are some exceptions. Now, take a look at the following chart to learn about some of the most common adverbs of manner:</p>"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":6,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Adverbs of manner\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Well\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Hard\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"So\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Quietly\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Quickly\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Accidently\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Closely\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Honestly\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Regularly\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Seriously\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "If you want, we can study together before the exam quickly."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Tomorrow, I have to study it carefully."}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "derive "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to have as origin of something "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: They derive from the Control class."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le01ob07aud01"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "query "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "instructions to know how to look for information in a database"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Plus, you can work with existing stores data and query, and write back to the file system."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le01ob07aud02"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "requirement "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "prerequisite to do something   "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You can tailor-make the object to meet the requirements of your application."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le01ob07aud03"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "tailor-make"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to customize "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You can tailor-make the object to meet the requirements of your application. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le01ob07aud04"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "file system "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "computer setup that keeps information from a data base "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Plus, you can work with existin