pdf", "content": "enpe7n5le01ob07re7pdf01.pdf"}]}]}], "template_override": 1, "type": "DWS"}], "stimuli": [{"type": "welcome", "id": 0, "contents": [{}], "_data": [], "template_override": 1}, {"id": 1, "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n5le01ob07re2img01.jpg", "text": "<strong>Melisa:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>I'm studying so hard, but I'm not ready for next Monday's exam. </span><br><strong>Anderson:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d> You will pass the exam. If you want, we can study together before the exam quickly. </span><br><strong>Melisa:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>Honestly, I don't have time next Monday, and that makes me feel under pressure; however, we can study now. </span><br><strong>Anderson:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d> Yes, ask me! </span><br>", "time": "0"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n5le01ob07re2img02.jpg", "text": "<strong>Melisa:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>What is a form in the .NET framework? </span><br><strong>Anderson:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d> A form is a bit of screen real state, but it's important to mention that in the .NET framework, it's a new platform for application development. You can use it to present information to the users and to accept input from them. Also, forms can be considered as standard windows, dialog boxes, multiple document interface (MDI), among others. </span><br><strong>Melisa:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>But, don't forget that in the .NET framework, forms are instances of classes too. You answered very well! Now, it's my turn! Tell me about some important features of forms. </span><br><strong>Anderson:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d> Forms are objects that show features that define their appearance and methods. By writing code to respond to its events, you can tailor-make the object to meet the requirements of your application. </span><br>", "time": "24000"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n5le01ob07re2img03.jpg", "text": "<strong>Melisa:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>And, what about the classes and controls? </span><br><strong>Anderson:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d> When you create the form with the Windows Forms Designer, it becomes a class. For that reason, when you display an instance of the form at run time, this class is the template used to create the form; moreover, forms are controls, as they derive from the Control class. </span><br><strong>Melisa:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>Tomorrow, I have to study it carefully. Finally, would you tell me why the form is the primary vehicle for interaction in the .NET framework? </span><br><strong>Anderson:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d> Fortunately, I know that one. It's the primary vehicle because you can get information from the user and answer to it; plus, you can work with existing stores data and query, and write back to the file system. </span><br><strong>Melisa:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>Very good!", "time": "86500"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n5le01ob07re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n5le01ob07re2aud01"}}], "_data": [], "title": "", "template_override": 1, "type": "audio"}], "_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}]}]}]}